    {
      "id": "s1_h2",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 16,
      "y": 346,
      "width": 688,
      "height": 34,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1f2937",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 128,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 129,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1708800000000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What is Wind Energy",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What is Wind Energy",
      "lineHeight": 1.4
    },
    {
      "id": "s1_b1",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 16,
      "y": 384,
      "width": 688,
      "height": 97,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1f2937",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 130,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 131,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1708800000000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "In reality, wind energy is a converted form of solar energy. The sun's radiation heats different\nparts of the earth at different rates, most notably during the day and night, but also when\ndifferent surfaces absorb or reflect at different rates. This, in turn, causes portions of the\natmosphere to warm differently. Hot air rises, reducing the atmospheric pressure at the\nearth's surface, and cooler air is drawn in to replace it. The result is wind.",
      "fontSize": 13,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "In reality, wind energy is a converted form of solar energy. The sun's radiation heats different\nparts of the earth at different rates, most notably during the day and night, but also when\ndifferent surfaces absorb or reflect at different rates. This, in turn, causes portions of the\natmosphere to warm differently. Hot air rises, reducing the atmospheric pressure at the\nearth's surface, and cooler air is drawn in to replace it. The result is wind.",
      "lineHeight": 1.4
    },
    {
      "id": "s1_b2",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 16,
      "y": 518,
      "width": 688,
      "height": 61,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1f2937",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 132,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 133,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1708800000000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Air has mass, and when it is in motion, it contains the energy of that motion (\"kinetic energy\").\nSome portion of that energy can be converted into other forms of mechanical force or electricity\nthat we can use to perform work.",
      "fontSize": 13,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Air has mass, and when it is in motion, it contains the energy of that motion (\"kinetic energy\").\nSome portion of that energy can be converted into other forms of mechanical force or electricity\nthat we can use to perform work.",
      "lineHeight": 1.4
    },
